 essentially blew up the Supreme  Court case what the cloud Act does is  that it essentially says that warrants  like the one issued to Microsoft in the  United States would apply to date a  sword stored overseas but the new law  lets tech companies challenge the  warrants in court if they think that  complying would break the law of the  country where the data is stored and I  guess that's a good thing but the thing  is that the law went further than that  it also allows foreign governments to  get the contents of communications  stored in the United States now an order  issued by a foreign government will have  to identify a specific person account  address or personal device and the order  has to be for a fixed limited amount of  time and one good thing here is that  these orders by foreign governments are  subject to review by our courts but the  question then becomes what foreign  countries would be eligible for this  partnership because not all countries  are great countries when it comes to  civil liberties human rights like who  can we trust  well that's where this law gets more  troubling because it allows the US  Justice Department to enter into  executive agreements with other  countries to set up this information  sharing relationship before the cloud  Act if a foreign government wanted to  get this kind of electronic data from  tech companies for an investigation they  would have had to have signed one of  those mutual legal assistance treaties  with the US government which requires  ratification by the US Senate but the  cloud act which is now law allows the  Attorney General almost single-handedly  to sign executive agreements with other  countries that would allow foreign  governments to get data hosted in the  United States just as long as their  target is not American or living in the  United States all the Attorney General  would have to do is submit a  certification to Congress that the  country is basically cool with civil  liberties but the cer